# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# search_documents — the PromptQL-facing Command
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This is the seam between PromptQL and the hybrid-retrieval backend.
#
# A Command (kind: Command, version: v1) exposes ONE function from a data
# connector to the Hasura DDN supergraph. Here it wraps the Python lambda
# connector's `search_documents` function (ddn/connector/search/functions.py),
# whose body is retrieval_bridge.search.RetrievalBridge.search_documents — the
# *same* code the local demo runs. Swap the backend (turbopuffer ⇄ LanceDB ⇄
# pgvector) and this metadata does not change: the command signature is the
# stable interface.
#
# HOW THIS FILE IS NORMALLY PRODUCED
#   `ddn connector-link add-resources <connector>` introspects the connector and
#   generates a first draft of this Command (and the SearchHit ObjectType in
#   SearchHit.hml). The connector function's docstring becomes the initial
#   `description`. You then HAND-TUNE the `description` below, because that text —
#   not the Python docstring — is what the PromptQL planner reads when deciding
#   whether to call this tool. Treat it as a tool spec written for the planner.
#
# WHY THE DESCRIPTION MATTERS (read this before editing)
#   Tool selection in PromptQL is driven by metadata `description` fields. If the
#   description is vague, the planner won't reach for hybrid retrieval when a user
#   asks "find tickets like this one" or "what past incidents match …". The text
#   below deliberately spells out: what it does, WHEN to use it, what the args
#   mean, and what comes back (including the `account` relationship to join).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

kind: Command
version: v1
definition:
  name: search_documents

  # ── Planner-facing tool spec. Multi-line, intentionally explicit. ──
  description: |
    Hybrid (semantic + exact-keyword) search over the support-ticket corpus.

    USE THIS to find tickets relevant to a natural-language description of an
    incident, question, or symptom — e.g. "past resolved incidents that look
    like a 503 storm after a deploy", "tickets mentioning ERR_DIM_384", or
    "anything about the connection pool on the scale plan". It is the right tool
    whenever the user wants to retrieve, look up, find similar, or gather past
    tickets before you reason over them.

    It runs BOTH a vector (semantic-meaning) sub-query AND a BM25 (exact-token)
    sub-query, then fuses them with reciprocal-rank fusion. That hybrid is the
    point: pure vector search misses exact tokens like error codes (ERR_DIM_384,
    CERT_EXPIRED), ticket IDs (TCK-10042), and plan tiers, while BM25 misses
    paraphrases — together they catch both. Prefer this over a plain text scan.

    Returns the top_k most relevant tickets as SearchHit rows (best first by the
    fused `score`). Each SearchHit carries flat, filterable attributes (status,
    plan_tier, error_code, severity, root_cause, component, created_at) and an
    `id` (the ticket id). Follow the `account` relationship on a SearchHit to
    join its structured account facts (project_name, plan_tier, monthly_revenue,
    region, seat_count) — use that to rank or prioritise hits by customer value.

    Typical plan: search_documents(...) to narrow the corpus to a handful of
    candidates, then classify/summarize each, join `account`, and rank.

  # ── Return type. A NON-NULL LIST of NON-NULL SearchHit. ──
  # SearchHit is the named ObjectType the connector return type maps to
  # (see SearchHit.hml). It MUST contain `id` so the `account` relationship
  # (relationship_searchhit_account.hml) can attach.
  outputType: "[SearchHit!]!"

  # ── Arguments exposed to the planner. These mirror the lambda signature:
  #    search_documents(query, top_k, plan_tier, status). The connector flattens
  #    plan_tier/status into the backend-agnostic `filters` dict internally; here
  #    they are first-class scalar args so the planner can set them directly. ──
  arguments:
    - name: query
      type: String!
      description: |
        Natural-language query describing the incident, symptom, or topic to
        find tickets about. Drives BOTH the semantic (vector) and exact-keyword
        (BM25) sub-queries — include any literal error codes or IDs verbatim so
        BM25 can match them exactly.

    - name: top_k
      type: Int
      description: |
        Maximum number of tickets to return, best-first by fused relevance.
        Defaults to 5 if omitted. Keep small (5–10): this step narrows the
        corpus to candidates worth reasoning over, not a full scan.

    - name: plan_tier
      type: String
      description: |
        Optional exact-match filter on the ticket's plan tier
        (one of: free, launch, scale, enterprise). Omit to search all tiers.

    - name: status
      type: String
      description: |
        Optional exact-match filter on ticket status (e.g. "resolved" or
        "open"). Set to "resolved" when the user wants past incidents that were
        actually fixed. Omit to search all statuses.

  # ── Where the function lives. `dataConnectorName` must match the connector's
  #    DataConnectorLink name; `function` is the connector function name as it
  #    appears in the introspected NDC schema. ──
  source:
    dataConnectorName: search
    dataConnectorCommand:
      function: search_documents
